H. Motion technology

Prefer:

CSS transitions.

CSS keyframes for simple effects.

Lightweight React animation patterns.

Framer Motion only where it materially improves the experience and does not add unnecessary bundle weight.

Do not introduce heavy animation libraries for simple transitions.

Do not use large Lottie files, unnecessary video backgrounds, WebGL scenes or continuously running canvas animations.

If SVG animation is used, keep it simple and lightweight.

I. Performance safeguards

Motion must not:

Delay page rendering.

Block user interactions.

Increase dashboard loading time.

Cause unnecessary React re-renders.

Animate thousands of table rows.

Continue running on hidden or off-screen content without a reason.

Consume excessive CPU or memory.

Interfere with chart interaction.

Affect financial calculations or background jobs.

Use lazy loading for non-essential visual assets.

Use animation only when relevant content enters the viewport.

Respect the user's prefers-reduced-motion setting. Provide reduced-motion alternatives.

On lower-powered devices, simplify or disable non-essential effects.

Keep the core platform fully usable when animations are disabled.

Final motion direction: Every interaction should feel intentional and responsive. The platform should look premium and smooth without looking over-animated.

